ical-model-and-machine-learning">Difference between Statistical Model and Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-ranking-algorithms-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Ranking Algorithms in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/multicollinearity-causes-effects-and-detection">Multicollinearity: Causes, Effects and Detection</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-n-grams-model">Bag of N-Grams Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tf-idf-model">TF-IDF Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-probabilistic-training-data-sampling-methods-in-machine-learning">5 Probabilistic Training Data Sampling Methods in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-websites-to-download-pre-trained-machine-learning-models">5 Websites to Download Pre-trained Machine Learning Models</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/a-comprehensive-comparison-of-ml-experiment-tracking-tools">A Comprehensive Comparison of ML Experiment Tracking Tools</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/adam-optimizer">Adam Optimizer</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solution-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solution in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solutions-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solutions in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/arithmetic-geometric-and-harmonic-mean">Arithmetic Geometric and Harmonic Mean</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/assumptions-of-logistic-regression">Assumptions of Logistic Regression</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/attention-mechanism-in-machine-learning">Attention Mechanism in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/autoencoder-classification-in-keras">Autoencoder Classification in Keras</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/automating-api-testing-with-postman">Automating API Testing With Postman</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-words-bow-model-in-machine-learning">Bag of Words (BoW) Model in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/batch-normalization">Batch Normalization</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-books-for-natural-language-processing-with-python">Best Books for Natural Language Processing With Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-computer-vision-books">Best Computer Vision Books</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bleu-score-in-machine-learning">Bleu Score in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/building-a-machine-learning-regression-model-from-a-multivariate-nonlinear-dataset">Building a Machine Learning Regression Model from a Multivariate Nonlinear Dataset</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/complex-multi-panel-figures">Complex Multi Panel Figures</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conformal-prediction-for-machine-learning-classification">Conformal Prediction for Machine Learning Classification</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-types-in-statistics-used-for-machine-learning">Data Types in Statistics Used for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/demographic-based-recommender-systems">Demographic-Based Recommender Systems</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/differences-between-machine-learning-engineers-and-researchers">Differences Between Machine Learning Engineers And Researchers</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/distance-measures-for-machine-learning">Distance Measures for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/early-stopping-in-machine-learning">Early Stopping in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/f2-score">F2 Score</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/gradient-boosting-algorithm">Gradient Boosting Algorithm</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-do-you-increase-statistical-power">How do you increase Statistical power</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-softmax-activation-function-works">How Softmax Activation Function Works</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-calculate-correlation-between-variables-in-python">How to Calculate Correlation Between Variables in Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-causality-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Causality in Machine Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading4"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse4" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse4" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading4" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse4"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-science">Data Science Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/nlp">NLP Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading5"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse5" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse5" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading5" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse5"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/machine-learning-interview-questions">Top 50 Machine learning Interview Questions and Answers (2025)</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="t-sne-in-machine-learning">next →</a> <a class="next" href="survival-analysis-using-machine-learning">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Numerical Solutions in Machine Learning</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>24 Jun 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 8 min read</span></div><p>Machine learning (ML) is fundamentally based on applying mathematical models to gain insights from data. These mathematical models and complex equations can rarely be formulated as analytical solutions, therefore numerical approximation must be utilized. The approximate solutions provided by numerical methods include solutions to optimization, regression, classification, clustering, and deep learning problems when an exact solution is unnecessary, impractical, or just impossible.</p><h2 class="h2">Why It is Important?</h2><p>Since closed-form solutions are not possible, numerical solutions play a critical role in developing <a href="machine-learning">machine learning</a> models grounded on more sophisticated real-world data. The necessity for approximated numerical solutions grows out of the inadequacies of analytical methods in data that are non-linear, noisy, and high-dimensional. Optimization problems are common in that you want to maximize or minimize a loss function, but the <a href="analytical-solution-in-machine-learning">analytical solution</a> is only possible with simple problems.</p><p>When the problem gets more complicated, as with <a href="deep-learning">deep neural networks</a> and support vector machines, analysis either becomes computationally expensive or inadequate. The type of numerical approximation method assists in the frustrations of finding optimal values using iterative solutions.</p><h2 class="h2">Important Numerical Methods</h2><p>There are various types of numerical methods that provide the foundation for tasks such as optimization, parameter estimation, and handling large datasets. Here are some of those:</p><h3 class="h3">01. Gradient Descent</h3><p>Gradient Descent is an important numerical optimization method in machine learning. It minimizes the loss function and thus the prediction error, by updating model parameters by stepping in the direction of the negative gradient of the <a href="pytorch-loss-function">loss function</a> to find an approximate minimum. Many machine learning models such as <a href="keras-artificial-neural-networks">neural networks</a>, <a href="logistic-regression-in-machine-learning">logistic regression</a>, and <a href="linear-regression-in-machine-learning">linear regression</a> rely on optimization, so gradient descent is useful. There are three kinds of gradient descent. Batch <a href="gradient-descent-in-machine-learning">Gradient Descent</a> computes the gradient (the average slope) over the entire dataset, presenting a stable but computationally heavy solution for large datasets, as it uses the entire dataset for each iteration.</p><p>Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) applies the update after evaluating the loss function for each data point in turn, so it is far less heavy on the memory, thus computation goes through faster, but there is noise introduced into the parameter updates. Mini-Batch Gradient Descent forms a solution using updates over small batches of data to create a middle-ground solution that is more stable than SGD updates but faster than updates over the entire dataset as in batch gradient descent.</p><h3 class="h3">02. Optimization Algorithms</h3><p>Optimization algorithms are important for addressing complex, non-linear problems, especially when gradient descent is not an appropriate method to use. Optimization methods provide multiple benefits including allowing a model to converge faster or enabling an optimization problem to be solved where second-order information is needed to compute the optimal solution to a concave function. Newton's Method can take advantage of second-order derivatives and the Hessian matrix to achieve very rapid convergence.</p><p>However, it is very computationally expensive for larger datasets. One solution to the computational burden is the Conjugate Gradient Method, which is a linear system efficient iteration in conjugate directions to reduce computation time. Quasi-Newton methods are any method that is similar to a Newton method but replaces second-order information with approximations of the Hessian matrix. The common quasi-Newton method is BFGS (Broyden-Fletcher-Goldfarb-Shanno) and its variant L-BFGS (Limited Memory BFGS).</p><p>Quasi-Newton methods become more computationally efficient than Newton methods, are well-respected methods of optimization, and BFGS/L-BFGS can function well in logistic regression or other large-scale optimization problems.</p><h3 class="h3">03. Numerical Integration</h3><p>We frequently necessitate numerical integration for machine learning models that are probabilistic and have some explicit probability distributions, such as in <a href="bayesian-optimization">Bayesian methods</a>. Most probabilistic models that we work with must compute likelihoods or posterior distributions, and these computations do not come with closed-form solutions. Among numerical methods, Monte Carlo integration is popular. Monte Carlo integration estimates integrals using random sampling, which works well for high-dimensional integrals.</p><p>We often use Monte Carlo integration, especially in Bayesian inference and with <a href="markov-chains">Markov Chain</a> Monte Carlo (MCMC) simulations. There are classical numerical integration methods such as Simpson's Rule and the Trapezoidal Rule, which you may have learned for continuous functions. These methods are not particularly useful for many complex machine learning problems, especially if they involve distributions as these techniques require some simpler structure than the samples being taken.</p><h3 class="h3">04. Root Finding Methods</h3><p>Root-finding methods are important for finding model parameters in regression as well as classification models, where solving equations is at the core of achieving model performance. The bisection method represents a simple bracketing method that repeatedly bisects intervals to locate roots. Although it is simple to implement, the convergence is slow. Hence, it is best used for root-locating contexts where precision is valued more than speed.</p><p>The Newton-Raphson method achieves much more rapid convergence as it takes into account the derivative of the function; however, the method is very sensitive to the initial guess, and a poor initial guess may result in the method diverging to less optimal results. The secant method improves on Newton-Raphson with the added advantage of not requiring second derivatives; it is computationally less expensive than bisection, while still achieving faster results than bisection.</p><p>Now we will do optimization algorithms for finding numerical solutions. For starters, A simple curve can help with visualization. For illustration, consider the graph of the function ( (x - 2)^2 ) as an intuitive example of a loss function. The layer outlines the approach of the gradient descent algorithm does indeed march step-by-step towards the minimum of the function.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from scipy.misc import derivative
from scipy import optimize
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
from numpy.linalg import norm

import warnings
warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')

plt.style.use('ggplot')


def f(x): return (x-2)**2  

x = range(-15,25)
y = [f(xn) for xn in x]

plt.plot(x, y, color='dodgerblue')
plt.title(r'f(x)=(x-2)^2')
plt.xlabel('x')
plt.ylabel('f(x)');
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Numerical Solutions in Machine Learning" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/machine-learning/images/numerical-solutions-in-machine-learning.png"/><p>Let's imagine choosing a point on a curve where ( x = -10 ) is our starting point. Let's say our learning rate is ( a = 0.1 ) and ( epsilon = 0.01 ). A point where the gradient equals zero is called a stationary point. At both of these points, the function provides no information about the behavior of the function and so once the algorithm reaches a stationary point we can stop the iteration process. The difference between the value and the updated value in each iteration will eventually be less than epsilon to indicate convergence and reaching the final value.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">prev_X = -10.0
step = 1e-1
eps = 1e-2

vals_X, vals_y = [], []

vals_X.append(prev_X)
vals_y.append(f(prev_X))

while True:
    current_x = prev_X - step*derivative(f, prev_X)
        
    vals_X.append(current_x)
    vals_y.append(f(current_x))
    
    if(current_x - prev_X)&lt;eps:
        break
        
    prev_X = current_x   
    
    
    
plt.plot(x, y, color='dodgerblue')
plt.plot(vals_X, vals_y, 'o', color='r', markerfacecolor='b', markeredgecolor='r')

plt.annotate('Starting point', xy=(-10, 144), xytext=(-10, 300), 
             bbox=dict(boxstyle='round', fc='lightblue', ec='gray'),
             arrowprops=dict(facecolor='black',  width=2, headwidth=6), )

plt.title(r'Gradient descent on f(x)=(x-2)^2')
plt.xlabel('x')
plt.ylabel('f(x)');
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Numerical Solutions in Machine Learning" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/machine-learning/images/numerical-solutions-in-machine-learning2.png"/><p>Let's imagine choosing a point on a curve where ( x = -10 ) is our starting point. Let's say our learning rate is ( a = 0.1 ) and ( epsilon = 0.01 ). A point where the gradient equals zero is called a stationary point. At both of these points, the function provides no information about the behavior of the function and so once the algorithm reaches a stationary point we can stop the iteration process. The difference between the value and the updated value in each iteration will eventually be less than epsilon to indicate convergence and reaching the final value.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">prev_X = -10.0
step = 1e-1
eps = 1e-2

vals_X, vals_y = [], []

vals_X.append(prev_X)
vals_y.append(f(prev_X))

while True:
    current_x = prev_X - step*derivative(f, prev_X)
        
    vals_X.append(current_x)
    vals_y.append(f(current_x))
    
    if(current_x - prev_X)&lt;eps:
        break
        
    prev_X = current_x   
    
    
    
plt.plot(x, y, color='dodgerblue')
plt.plot(vals_X, vals_y, 'o', color='r', markerfacecolor='b', markeredgecolor='r')

plt.annotate('Starting point', xy=(-10, 144), xytext=(-10, 300), 
             bbox=dict(boxstyle='round', fc='lightblue', ec='gray'),
             arrowprops=dict(facecolor='black',  width=2, headwidth=6), )

plt.title(r'Gradient descent on f(x)=(x-2)^2')
plt.xlabel('x')
plt.ylabel('f(x)');
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Numerical Solutions in Machine Learning" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/machine-learning/images/numerical-solutions-in-machine-learning3.png"/><p>During each iteration, the algorithm modifies the parameter values and slowly converges to the bottom. The absolute highest and lowest values of the function are called the global maximum and global minimum, respectively. The other stationary points are known as local maximum and local minimum points. The aim of gradient descent is to get to the global minimum, but it may be possible that the process falls into a local minimum instead.</p><p>The function can have multiple global minima and maxima known as extrema. Furthermore, a 0 gradient does not always guarantee there is an extremum since there are also saddle points that have a gradient of 0 or near and are not optimal. This sequence of events can make the optimization problem more difficult, and many researchers have investigated other ways to improve the optimization problem.</p><p>The term Mini-Batch Gradient Descent refers to when instead of a single data point, we randomly select a subset of data at each iteration. The way Mini-Batch Gradient Descent works is that we calculate the gradient with respect to each subset, and the algorithm then updates parameters in the opposite direction of the gradient. This stochastic process, relative to each iteration, decreases computational cost per iteration but may yield noisy estimates for the gradients thus resulting in oscillation near a minimum.</p><p>To illustrate the difference between Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) and Mini-Batch SGD consider plotting their gradient paths on a plot while being given random sample data. The following graph indicates how SGD and Mini-Batch SGD would behave while converging toward the minimum in plotting two subplots. In the subplots, you may indicate the contour lines of the loss function that can be interpreted as common representations of a 3D surface plotted on a 2D figure.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def plot_generation(batch_size, title, ax=None):
    
    if ax is None:
        ax = plt.gca()
        
    np.random.seed(2)
    point_targ = np.random.normal(4, 2, 2)
    point_current = np.random.normal(6, 2, 2)
    X = np.random.normal(2, 3, (100, 2))
    y = np.dot(X, point_targ) + np.random.normal(0, 1, 100)
    
    eps = 1e-2
    lr = 1e-2
    history = []
    
    history.append(point_current.copy())
    point_prev = point_current + 2 * eps

    while norm(point_current - point_prev) &gt; eps:
        point_prev = point_current.copy()

        indices = np.random.choice(X.shape[0], batch_size, replace=False)
        batch_X, batch_y = X[indices], y[indices]

        point_current -= 2 * lr * np.dot(batch_X.T, np.dot(batch_X, point_current) - batch_y) / batch_y.shape[0]
        history.append(point_current.copy())
           
    
    D1, D2 = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(-1, 10, 100), np.linspace(-1, 10, 100))
    D = np.dstack((D1, D2))
    Z = np.zeros_like(D1)
    
    for i in range(D1.shape[0]):
        for j in range(D1.shape[1]):
            Z[i, j] = np.mean(np.power(np.dot(X, D[i][j]) - y, 2))

            
    ax.set_title(str(title))
    ax.set_xlim((-1, 8))
    ax.set_ylim((0, 10))

    ax.contour(D1, D2, Z, levels=np.linspace(0, 1000, 30),  cmap=plt.cm.plasma)
    ax.plot(point_targ[0], point_targ[1], 'rx', markersize=12)
    ax.plot(np.array(history)[:, 0], np.array(history)[:, 1], 
            marker='&lt;', color='dodgerblue', markersize=10, 
            markeredgecolor='purple', linewidth=4)
    
    ax.annotate('Starting point', xy=(-0.5, 9), size=15, 
                bbox=dict(boxstyle='round', fc='lightblue', ec='gray'))
    
    ax.set_xlabel('Weight_1')
    ax.set_ylabel('Weight_2');
    return ax
    

fig, axes = plt.subplots(1,2, figsize=(16,6), sharey=True)

titles = ['SGD', 'Mini-Batch SGD, batch size=10']
for i, ax in enumerate(fig.axes):
    ax=plot_generation(batch_size=np.power(10, i), title=titles[i],  ax=ax)
    
plt.tight_layout(w_pad=2)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Numerical Solutions in Machine Learning" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/machine-learning/images/numerical-solutions-in-machine-learning4.png"/><p>Numerical methods form the backbone of machine learning, albeit with challenges that must be addressed relating to computational time and resources, numerical stability, and the convergence of algorithms. These challenges are magnified with high-dimensional datasets and non-linear models in more complex settings and necessitate the development of advanced optimization techniques, stochastic methods, and numerical <a href="matlab-linear-algebra">linear algebra</a>.</p><p>Future communities of research will focus on research aimed at improving the speed of convergence, minimizing computational cost, and improving convergence rates; as well as developing hybrid analytics methods to improve performance Both distributed computing and quantum computing have the potential to radically change the way and speed in which numerical solutions can be found, with the availability of significant processing power of massive datasets.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="t-sne-in-machine-learning">t-SNE in Machine Learning</a></span></div><br/> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="survival-analysis-using-machine-learning" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="t-sne-in-machine-learning" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/> </td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="how-to-get-model-summary-in-pytorch"><h3 class="h3">How to get Model Summary in PyTorch</h3><p class="related-post-content">? It is imperative to print a model summary to know about the structure and parameters of a neural network. Although Keras has a basi